Forecast a judgement before writing one, and measure P-280 on the real forest (P-280)

A judging run of the production forest would write 136.5 GiB describing 18.67 GB of
evidence, and 72% of that is one object name copied three times, 331 million times. A
real run was abandoned after eight hours at 27 GB still growing, so the size is the run
time here and not only the disk.

The specification asked for a read-only forecast before any schema change, because the
owner works only inside the production environment and a scope chosen by guesswork
costs him a day. This is that forecast, plus what it found and what an outside review
then found in the proposal built on it.

SecureAD.Measure --judgement-forecast opens a bundle read-only, writes to no database
in it, and reports the exact occurrence count, the distinguished-name lengths and what
four candidate storage shapes would cost.

IT COUNTS STORED ENTRIES, NOT PROJECTED ONES, and that is the whole correctness of it.
descriptor_entry and captured_object.ace_count are one cheap column away and hold the
canonical projection, sorted and deduplicated by ace_hash. Judging writes one row per
entry re-parsed from security_descriptor.raw_bytes, where nothing is dropped and
nothing is reordered; a lab bundle measured 12,603 stored against 12,600 projected. A
forecast reading the cheap column would have agreed with itself, reported a plausible
figure and been short by exactly what deduplication removed - which is the shape of
defect that passes while measuring nothing. The specification's own ~344 million
estimate was built on that column and is corrected here.

CHECKED AGAINST A REAL JUDGEMENT. On next/artifacts/p279-after it forecast 17,164,866
occurrences against that judgement's actual 17,164,866 rows, and 46,985 object rows
against 46,985 captured objects. Without that the projection would be a calculation
nobody could contradict.

Measured on ad.ssa.gov-20260924-020431: 940,067 objects, 659,196 carrying readable
permissions, 330,856,576 occurrences, names averaging 106.1 bytes, 238,118,809
occurrences carrying an object-type GUID and 205,792,883 an inherited one. Keyed per
descriptor with the entry-derived columns moved onto distinct entries, the occurrence
table models at 6.9 GiB against 136.5.

THE REVIEW DID NOT LET THAT PROPOSAL THROUGH, and it was right. permission_entry
already carries target-dependent trustee presentation: WellKnownTrustee renders
S-1-5-10 as "This object (Alice)" and SharedPermissionStorage writes it into the row.
Sharing one entry per descriptor would have displayed Alice's name while reading Bob -
a permission attributed to the wrong object, which is the one thing an audit tool must
never do. Nothing shipped does this, because ContentKey serialises the whole entry
including that name so the two never share; the comment above it nevertheless asserts
"The target never enters it", and the proposal was built on that stated contract rather
than on the code. Recorded as P-285 along with its live cost - a SELF entry therefore
never deduplicates, on the commonest ACE in Active Directory.

Three more findings recorded rather than fixed here: P-283, an occurrence is bound to
its entry but not to its interpretation, so a reading_id can be repointed at another
valid reading and an allow WRITE_DAC reads as "A deny entry grants no access"; P-284,
this forecast counts objects with unparseable descriptors as having readable
permissions, reporting "could not compute" as "found nothing"; and two of my own
claims withdrawn - the "1.3% agreement between independent methods" was cancellation,
54N - 36G accounting for the gap exactly, and the 3.5 GB figure is produced by no
implemented model.

P-282 is fixed here. SecureAD.Measure ran the Self-Membership sweep whenever it did not
recognise what it was asked for, so a mistyped flag started a twenty-minute synthetic
benchmark that printed plausible progress while taking no measurement; it cost the
owner a production session. Now a positive test - only a leading repeat count asks for
the sweep - and anything else prints the modes and exits 1 naming the unrecognised
word.

Two defects found by running the build the owner would actually carry rather than the
one on the bench: provenance hashed the apphost, which is byte-identical between Debug
and Release and so named no build at all; and hashing the managed assembly instead
crashed under a single-file publish, where Assembly.Location is empty. Both branches
are now chosen explicitly, reported, and tested.

27 tests. Each behaviour check mutation-tested with explicit rebuilds before and after
restoring: counting descriptor_entry instead of parsing raw_bytes, length() instead of
length(CAST(.. AS BLOB)), dropping the descriptor-reuse multiplier, charging the name
per object rather than per occurrence, and restoring the sweep fall-through. Each
killed only its intended check.

The report says "Opened read-only" rather than "nothing was written", because a
forecast of p279-after left both databases byte for byte identical and both -shm files
carrying a new timestamp. Small, but an audit tool is not the thing that rounds a claim
up.

docs/specs/P280-PROPOSAL.md is frozen and carries a do-not-build banner.
docs/specs/P280-REVIEW-PROMPT.md is what was handed to the reviewer. The living
specification is P280-JUDGEMENT-OCCURRENCE-ROWS.md and where they disagree it wins.
